<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # 1 – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Visiting Our Country</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Unique Facts About My Area</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Analyze the tone and implied meaning in discussions. Interactive: Respond appropriately when participating in discussions on familiar topics.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can recognize tone and implied meaning in conversations about visitor safety by identifying key concerns and suggested precautions (e.g., "They said Panama is generally safe, but emphasized being cautious in crowded areas, which suggests concern about pickpocketing."). Can identify key attractions from a conversation about a city tour (e.g., "They said the Panama Canal, the Contemporary Art Museum, and the local beaches are good places to see!"). Can interpret implied suggestions and respond appropriately when discussing travel plans (e.g., "The guide suggested taking a bus because it makes it easy to get around in the city.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333; background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing pictures of famous landmarks and unique places in Panama (e.g., Casco Viejo, Panama Canal, Biomuseo, local beaches). Students are asked to briefly share if they have visited these places and what they found interesting or unique about them. (e.g., "Have you visited the Panama Canal? What did you like about it?") This activates prior knowledge and introduces the theme. </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models key vocabulary for describing attractions and safety (e.g., <i>attraction, landmark, guide, precaution, safety, recommend, must-see, interesting, unique, breathtaking</i>) by describing a local spot using these words. For example: "The BioMuseo is a <b>unique attraction</b> in Panama City; it's a <b>must-see landmark</b>. Our local <b>guide</b> always <b>recommends</b> it. You should take a <b>precaution</b> and arrive early. It's a very <b>interesting</b> place." The teacher explicitly models the pronunciation of contrastive sounds like /st/ in "stay" and /ks/ in "explain," and reduced syllables in "interesting" (/ˈɪn.trə.stɪŋ/) and "restaurant" (/ˈres.trɒnt/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the vocabulary and pronunciation.
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If a place is a 'must-see,' does it mean many people visit it or few people know about it?" (Expected answer: Many people visit it.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is the difference between an 'attraction' and a 'precaution'?" (Expected answer: An attraction is a place to visit; a precaution is something you do to stay safe.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Students will listen to an audio recording of a conversation (approx. 2-3 minutes) between a foreign tourist and a local Panamanian guide discussing potential attractions, local unique facts, safety tips, and transportation options for a city tour in Panama. The conversation naturally incorporates the target grammar (Past simple/Present perfect, Future with 'will'/'going to', Reported speech) and vocabulary (<i>area, location, attraction, plan, market, museum, restaurant, beach, history, landmark, guide, itinerary, precaution, safety, transportation, visit, explore, recommend, advise, breathtaking, tranquil, interesting, unique, wonderful, must-see, local, safe</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Analyze the tone and implied meaning in discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Listening:</b> After the first listen, students answer: "What is the main purpose of this conversation?" and "Are the speakers talking about future plans or past events?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Detailed Listening:</b> Students listen again and complete True/False statements based on specific information from the dialogue. Example statements: "The guide recommended visiting the fish market for lunch." (True/False). "The tourist has already visited Casco Viejo several times." (True/False). "The guide suggested taking a taxi everywhere for safety." (True/False – implied meaning).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Interpreting Implied Meaning:</b> Students identify the speaker's tone or implied meaning for specific phrases. E.g., The guide says, "Panama is generally safe, but it's always wise to be cautious in crowded areas like the market." What does the guide imply? (Concern about pickpocketing).</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students will practice identifying key information, specific grammatical structures (Past simple and Present perfect, Future with 'will' and 'going to', Reported speech), and target vocabulary (<i>visit, area, location, attraction, plan, direction, market, museum, restaurant, beach, town, history, blocks, cathedral, accommodations, landmark, guide, itinerary, precaution, safety, transportation, see, explore, showcase, witness, connect, miss, recommend, give, explain, advise, arrange, breathtaking, tranquil, interesting, unique, wonderful, must-see, local, national, famous, close, available, safe, go straight, turn right, turn left, across from, next to, between, behind, opposite, in front of</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Gap-fill and Matching:</b> Students receive a partial transcript of the audio with blanks for key vocabulary words (e.g., <i>attraction, precaution, recommend, safe, unique</i>) and listen again to fill in the gaps. They then match phrases from the audio to their implied meanings (e.g., "be cautious in crowded areas" matches "concern about pickpocketing").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Reported Speech Transformation:</b> Students are given direct quotes from the audio (e.g., Guide: "You should definitely visit the Panama Canal.") and transform them into reported speech. (e.g., "The guide <b>recommended</b> that the tourist <b>should visit</b> the Panama Canal." or "The guide <b>said</b> that the tourist <b>was going to visit</b> the Panama Canal."). Another activity could involve transforming sentences using Past Simple and Present Perfect (e.g., "Casco Viejo <b>was</b> once neglected, but it <b>has become</b> popular.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pair Interaction - Discussion & Verification:</b> In pairs, students discuss specific recommendations or safety tips mentioned in the audio. They confirm what they heard and how they interpreted the tone. For example, "What did the guide say about transportation? Did they explicitly recommend a bus, or did they imply it was easy to use?" They also practice pronunciation of words like "restaurant" and "interesting".</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Paired Role-Play: "Local Guide and Curious Tourist".</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Description:</b> Students work in pairs. One student takes on the role of a tourist visiting Panama for the first time, interested in "Unique Facts About My Area." The other student acts as a local guide. The tourist asks for recommendations on local attractions, unique experiences, and safety advice. The guide listens carefully to the tourist's interests, offers suggestions, gives directions, and provides important precautions, using the vocabulary and grammar practiced. The tourist must listen attentively to understand the recommendations, the implied meaning of safety tips, and respond appropriately, confirming understanding or asking for clarification. Students will focus on asking for recommendations (e.g., "What places should my friend visit?") and describing popular landmarks (e.g., "The museum showcases our cultural heritage."). They will also practice planning events and offering suggestions clearly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in understanding and responding to recommendations and safety advice, collaborative interaction, and successful completion of the communication task (planning a mini-itinerary and providing/receiving advice). The activity requires active listening to both explicit information and implied suggestions, and then formulating appropriate verbal responses.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the role-play, focusing on the tourist's listening comprehension and the guide's clarity and use of appropriate vocabulary/grammar when giving advice.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students perform their "Local Guide and Curious Tourist" role-play for the teacher or a small group. The teacher observes the tourist's ability to interpret implied suggestions and respond appropriately to safety advice and recommendations, and the guide's ability to clearly communicate information and suggestions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Clear articulation of words, especially target sounds /st/, /ks/, /tʃ/, /ʃ/ and reduced syllables (e.g., "interesting," "restaurant").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Appropriate use of words related to attractions, safety, directions (e.g., <i>attraction, precaution, recommend, safe, must-see, go straight, next to</i>).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Interpretation:</b> Tourist's ability to demonstrate understanding of both explicit and implied information/suggestions (e.g., responding to a subtle warning about crowded areas, understanding a preference for a specific type of attraction).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Interaction:</b> Active listening, appropriate turn-taking, and relevant contributions to the conversation.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was one new thing you learned about giving/receiving travel advice today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How confident do you feel about understanding implied meanings in conversations now (1-5 scale)?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was challenging about analyzing implied meanings in the listening activities, and how did you try to overcome it?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What specific vocabulary or phrases about local attractions and safety do you feel more confident using or understanding now?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The listening activities were effective in introducing the core vocabulary and grammar through an authentic context. Students demonstrated good comprehension of explicit information, but some found interpreting subtle implied meanings more challenging, particularly around safety precautions. I need to provide more explicit examples and guided practice on identifying tone and subtle suggestions in future listening tasks. The pronunciation practice on reduced syllables was helpful, but some students still need more reinforcement. For the next lesson, I will ensure the reading text clearly reiterates these vocabulary words and grammatical structures, providing additional context and opportunities for comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson introduced core vocabulary and concepts about local attractions, planning, and safety through listening. In Lesson 2 (Reading Comprehension), students will read a text (e.g., a travel blog or article) describing "Unique Facts About My Area" which will reinforce the same vocabulary and grammatical structures, allowing them to see these words in a different context and further develop their receptive skills.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>